WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.441
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.441 --> 00:00:06.194
coordinate a producer and consumer through a

00:00:06.194 --> 00:00:09.010
bounded queue and use an agreed item count to

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.512
complete their delivery.

00:00:11.152 --> 00:00:13.740
Separating delivery from processing helps

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.708
applications handle requests at different rates

00:00:16.708 --> 00:00:19.486
while limiting pending work and establishing

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:21.127
when a report is complete.

00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:24.645
A campus supply desk passes item requests from

00:00:24.645 --> 00:00:26.019
one worker to another.

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:28.392
Our first list contains tag, then map.

00:00:28.392 --> 00:00:31.265
The producer must send each name once, and the

00:00:31.265 --> 00:00:33.826
consumer must collect both in that order.

00:00:33.826 --> 00:00:36.449
The queue can hold two pending names; each

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:38.697
removal makes space available again.

00:00:39.348 --> 00:00:42.009
We expect the received list to contain tag, then

00:00:42.009 --> 00:00:44.282
map, and the completed queue to be empty.

00:00:44.282 --> 00:00:46.611
Both workers must finish before the caller

00:00:46.611 --> 00:00:47.775
prints those reports.

00:00:47.775 --> 00:00:50.159
Later, you'll predict a delivery with three

00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:52.488
names through the same capacity-two queue.

00:00:52.488 --> 00:00:54.982
We will distinguish what the queue guarantees

00:00:54.982 --> 00:00:57.588
from what the final output cannot tell us about

00:00:57.588 --> 00:00:58.198
scheduling.

00:00:58.881 --> 00:01:01.320
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.208
We'll build the two jobs, connect them to one

00:01:04.208 --> 00:01:06.840
queue, and have the caller wait for their

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:07.931
completed report.

00:01:11.755 --> 00:01:14.534
We need ArrayList for the consumer's result and

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:16.840
two queue types for the shared handoff.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.146
BlockingQueue describes the operations;

00:01:19.146 --> 00:01:21.984
ArrayBlockingQueue supplies their fixed-capacity

00:01:21.984 --> 00:01:22.871
implementation.

00:01:22.871 --> 00:01:25.472
Let's add those imports and the main method.

00:01:25.472 --> 00:01:28.311
The caller declares InterruptedException because

00:01:28.311 --> 00:01:30.380
its later joins can be interrupted.

00:01:50.312 --> 00:01:53.242
Let's define WordDelivery, the producer's job.

00:01:53.242 --> 00:01:56.108
It keeps the supplied queue reference and the

00:01:56.108 --> 00:01:57.064
array of names.

00:01:57.064 --> 00:02:00.057
Recording those references does not copy either

00:02:00.057 --> 00:02:00.503
object.

00:02:00.503 --> 00:02:03.306
Our example leaves the input array unchanged

00:02:03.306 --> 00:02:04.962
while the worker reads it.

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:39.010
The producer's run method will visit every name

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.634
in array order and call put once for each.

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:44.572
Put can wait when the bounded queue has no free

00:02:44.572 --> 00:02:47.197
slot, so we'll place the loop inside a try

00:02:47.197 --> 00:02:47.822
statement.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.742
A full queue slowing delivery is backpressure.

00:03:02.742 --> 00:03:05.515
Put can proceed when a removal makes space, so

00:03:05.515 --> 00:03:08.227
the capacity limits pending names rather than

00:03:08.227 --> 00:03:09.794
the total names delivered.

00:03:09.794 --> 00:03:12.687
Let's also handle an interrupted queue operation

00:03:12.687 --> 00:03:15.399
by restoring this worker's interrupt flag and

00:03:15.399 --> 00:03:16.845
reaching the end of run.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.604
InterruptedException reports a cooperative

00:03:32.604 --> 00:03:34.626
interruption and clears the flag.

00:03:34.626 --> 00:03:37.383
Restoring it records the request; leaving the

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:39.650
work method is what ends this worker.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.530
The expected report follows normal delivery, so

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:45.410
this exit path should not be mistaken for proof

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.555
that every requested name was sent.

00:03:47.586 --> 00:03:49.596
Now let's define WordCollector.

00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:52.448
It needs the same queue, an agreed number of

00:03:52.448 --> 00:03:55.495
items to receive, and its own ArrayList for the

00:03:55.495 --> 00:03:55.949
report.

00:03:55.949 --> 00:03:59.061
Only this consumer will change that result list.

00:04:36.638 --> 00:04:39.438
Let's implement the collector's counted loop.

00:04:39.438 --> 00:04:42.301
Take removes and returns the next queued item,

00:04:42.301 --> 00:04:44.106
waiting if none is available.

00:04:44.106 --> 00:04:47.093
Java obtains that returned name before adding it

00:04:47.093 --> 00:04:50.080
to received; the add has not occurred while take

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.138
is still waiting.

00:05:10.628 --> 00:05:13.271
ArrayBlockingQueue uses first-in, first-out

00:05:13.271 --> 00:05:13.640
order.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.590
With one producer and one consumer, the received

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.172
names keep the producer's insertion order.

00:05:19.172 --> 00:05:21.938
A take transfers its item to one receiver; it

00:05:21.938 --> 00:05:23.291
does not broadcast it.

00:05:23.291 --> 00:05:26.118
Let's give the collector the same interruption

00:05:26.118 --> 00:05:28.946
response and a report method for its completed

00:05:28.946 --> 00:05:29.253
list.

00:05:48.946 --> 00:05:51.814
The two roles own different surrounding state:

00:05:51.814 --> 00:05:54.744
the producer reads the unchanged input, and the

00:05:54.744 --> 00:05:56.677
consumer builds its own result.

00:05:56.677 --> 00:05:59.358
The queue coordinates their shared handoff.

00:05:59.358 --> 00:06:02.039
It does not automatically protect arbitrary

00:06:02.039 --> 00:06:04.346
objects elsewhere in the application.

00:06:04.408 --> 00:06:07.384
Let's create the first input, tag followed by

00:06:07.384 --> 00:06:10.558
map, and a queue that stores at most two pending

00:06:10.558 --> 00:06:10.955
names.

00:06:10.955 --> 00:06:14.064
This number describes the queue's capacity, not

00:06:14.064 --> 00:06:15.783
a lifetime delivery limit.

00:06:28.005 --> 00:06:30.929
Now let's connect both jobs to that one queue.

00:06:30.929 --> 00:06:33.217
WordDelivery receives the array, and

00:06:33.217 --> 00:06:35.377
WordCollector receives its length.

00:06:35.377 --> 00:06:38.110
This is the finite completion protocol: the

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:41.161
number of successful takes must match the number

00:06:41.161 --> 00:06:42.305
of supplied names.

00:06:56.857 --> 00:06:59.514
An empty queue between handoffs does not mean

00:06:59.514 --> 00:07:02.290
delivery is over; the producer might still have

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:03.530
another name to send.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:06.010
The agreed count supplies the ending rule.

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:08.727
An extra take could wait for an item that will

00:07:08.727 --> 00:07:11.384
never arrive, while too few takes could leave

00:07:11.384 --> 00:07:12.388
work uncollected.

00:07:12.388 --> 00:07:14.632
Let's wrap each job in its own Thread.

00:07:23.029 --> 00:07:25.530
Both roles need a chance to make progress.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.328
Let's start both workers before joining either.

00:07:28.328 --> 00:07:31.126
Starting a producer and immediately waiting for

00:07:31.126 --> 00:07:33.806
it could prevent the caller from starting the

00:07:33.806 --> 00:07:36.604
consumer that would free space in a full queue.

00:07:42.853 --> 00:07:44.587
Now let's join both workers.

00:07:44.587 --> 00:07:46.939
Normal returns establish that each has

00:07:46.939 --> 00:07:49.787
terminated and that the caller can observe its

00:07:49.787 --> 00:07:50.592
earlier work.

00:07:50.592 --> 00:07:53.564
The caller can then read the consumer-owned list

00:07:53.564 --> 00:07:56.536
and inspect whether this completed delivery left

00:07:56.536 --> 00:07:57.403
a queued item.

00:08:02.708 --> 00:08:05.421
Let's print the received list and the queue's

00:08:05.421 --> 00:08:07.591
empty check, then close the program.

00:08:07.591 --> 00:08:10.243
Neither report will tell us whether a worker

00:08:10.243 --> 00:08:13.137
actually waited, which worker finished first, or

00:08:13.137 --> 00:08:15.608
whether the two roles ran simultaneously.

00:08:28.773 --> 00:08:31.756
Before running, connect the rules: two names are

00:08:31.756 --> 00:08:34.676
put in order, two names are taken in order, and

00:08:34.676 --> 00:08:36.602
both joins precede the reports.

00:08:36.602 --> 00:08:39.523
Let's click Run to check the complete delivery.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:49.253
The collector received tag, then map, and the

00:08:49.253 --> 00:08:50.210
queue is empty.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:52.764
The two handoffs match the agreed count.

00:08:52.764 --> 00:08:55.318
This is evidence of the completed finite

00:08:55.318 --> 00:08:58.254
delivery, while the final state alone does not

00:08:58.254 --> 00:09:01.255
reveal an earlier full or empty waiting period.

00:09:01.363 --> 00:09:04.315
Let's change only the input array to pen, map,

00:09:04.315 --> 00:09:04.892
and book.

00:09:04.892 --> 00:09:06.881
Keep the queue capacity at two.

00:09:06.881 --> 00:09:09.833
Because the collector takes the array's actual

00:09:09.833 --> 00:09:12.720
length, the next fresh run will require three

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:15.351
receives without changing either worker's

00:09:15.351 --> 00:09:16.313
implementation.

00:09:22.278 --> 00:09:24.941
Predict both output lines before running.

00:09:24.941 --> 00:09:27.540
Explain how three items can pass through

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.138
capacity two, and whether the report can

00:09:30.138 --> 00:09:32.996
establish that the producer actually waited.

00:09:32.996 --> 00:09:35.920
Use item order, removal making space, and the

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:38.453
agreed receive count in your reasoning.

00:09:46.765 --> 00:09:49.606
Let's click Run to test the three-name delivery

00:09:49.606 --> 00:09:51.540
through the same queue capacity.

00:09:58.156 --> 00:10:00.776
All three names arrived in order, and the

00:10:00.776 --> 00:10:02.374
completed queue is empty.

00:10:02.374 --> 00:10:04.930
Removing names makes space available, so

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:07.998
capacity two does not restrict total delivery to

00:10:07.998 --> 00:10:08.254
two.

00:10:08.254 --> 00:10:11.066
The output still does not establish an exact

00:10:11.066 --> 00:10:13.750
worker schedule or prove that either queue

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:15.156
operation had to wait.

00:10:15.258 --> 00:10:17.655
You coordinated two roles with one bounded queue

00:10:17.655 --> 00:10:19.103
and a finite completion rule.

00:10:19.103 --> 00:10:21.500
In an ongoing application, stopping one role can

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:22.699
leave the other waiting.

00:10:22.699 --> 00:10:24.846
Its owner must request interruption of both

00:10:24.846 --> 00:10:27.044
before joining either, and both workers must

00:10:27.044 --> 00:10:27.993
respond by exiting.

00:10:27.993 --> 00:10:30.390
For a transfer task, describe an ordered request

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:32.587
stream, choose what limits pending work, and

00:10:32.587 --> 00:10:34.735
distinguish a fully delivered report from a

00:10:34.735 --> 00:10:35.983
completed early shutdown.
